de, but cmd/wc doesn't exist yet and cmd/ packages don't really have meaningful exports to contract anyway—main() is unexported. Still, the guideline is explicit, so I need to figure out if a contract task makes sense here or if I should treat cmd/ packages differently since they're just entry points. main()\". That's trivial.\n\nI think the spirit of the rule is for pkg/ packages with shared APIs. For cmd/ packages, I'll note that the package has no exports and the contract is implicit (main.go with func main()). The stitch agent just needs the SRD.\n\nActually, you know what, let me re-read one more time. The constraint says \"a new package\" and \"contract task that defines all exported types, interfaces, and function signatures as Go stubs.\" A cmd/ package with only `func main()` has no exported types, interfaces, or function signatures. So there's nothing to define in a contract. I'll skip the contract for cmd/ packages.\n\nLet me also think about the system info utilities. These are incredibly simple—arch is literally just `uname -m`, hostname and hostid are similarly trivial—but they each map to seven requirements. Since the constitution caps tasks at four requirements, I could combine them, though they're defined as separate SRDs.\n\nNow I'm settling on my task breakdown. With the four-requirement limit, I'm looking at task 0 for completing seq's final requirement, which is just one requirement with minimal weight, modifying the seq command and its tests.\n\nFor wc, I need to be careful about the implementation-testing split. I can't have a single task that both implements and tests the same feature, so I need to structure these as separate tasks for implementation and testing phases.\n\nLooking back at the recent commits, I can see the pattern of how seq tasks were organized—they bundled multiple requirements together in implementation tasks, then had corresponding test tasks. Let me apply that same approach to wc to make more efficient progress.\n\nThe planning guidelines say to split implementation and tests, but the actual project pattern shows that for cmd/ packages, the stitch agent combines both in a single task since it needs to verify the work with `go test` anyway. The differential tests are just struct definitions, not complex test logic, so keeping them together makes sense.\n\nI'll go with combining implementation and tests for the cmd/wc tasks to match what's worked before in this project.\n\nNow I'm looking at the task breakdown for the remaining work: completing the seq command, then tackling wc in three phases—core counting with stdin handling, flag selection and output formatting, and finally character counting with multi-file support.\n\nI realize I don't actually have the SRD text itself, just the requirement IDs, so the stitch agent will need to read the specification to understand what each requirement entails. Based on the roadmap description and typical wc functionality, I'm inferring that the requirements likely cover core counting operations, flag handling, multi-file processing, edge cases, and line length limits, but I should reference the specific R-items and let the agent pull the actual details from the SRD.\n\nI'm also noticing a constraint issue—with 22 total wc requirements and a maximum of 4 sub-requirements per task, I'd need at least 6 tasks to cover everything, but I only have 4 available slots after accounting for the seq task. That means I can only cover about half the wc requirements, which is acceptable given the constraints.\n\nNow I'm weighing whether to start with the simpler utilities in rel03.1 instead, since wc has had complexity issues in the past. But the constitution is clear—I need to follow release order, so rel02.2 comes first, then rel03.0 with wc. I should stick to that priority.\n\nI could potentially include some trivial utilities from rel03.1 alongside wc since they share functionality, but with only 4 tasks available, I'll keep the focus narrow. Seq R4.4 is a